{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":25506,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000008","total_cost_usd":0.16031649999999997,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12256,"cache_read_input_tokens":13793,"output_tokens":1209,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12256,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":540,"outputTokens":17,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.000625,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5[1m]":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":1209,"cacheReadInputTokens":13793,"cacheCreationInputTokens":12256,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.1596915,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"# The Semicolon: A Brief History and Grammar\n\nThe semicolon arrived in print in 1494, in Venice, when the humanist printer Aldus Manutius issued Pietro Bembo's *De Aetna* in a new roman type cut by Francesco Griffo. The mark was a deliberate invention: a compound of the colon's stacked gravity and the comma's tail, meant to sit in the gap between them. Renaissance punctuation was largely rhetorical — it told a reader aloud how long to pause — and the semicolon filled an audible middle distance: longer than a comma's breath, shorter than a colon's stop.\n\nAdoption was gradual. Italian printers took it up over the following decades, and it reached England by the late sixteenth century, where Ben Jonson's *English Grammar* (printed 1640) gave it something like official standing. As prose became a thing read silently rather than performed, punctuation drifted from breath toward structure, and the semicolon acquired the grammatical duties it now holds. Nineteenth-century writers used it lavishly — Melville and Dickens built long articulated sentences on it — while the twentieth century grew suspicious. Kurt Vonnegut called semicolons \"transvestite hermaphrodites representing absolutely nothing,\" advice that says more about mid-century plain style than about the mark itself.\n\nIts modern grammar is narrow and precise. Chiefly, a semicolon joins two independent clauses — each capable of standing alone — whose relationship is close enough that a period would sever it: *The rain stopped; the road stayed flooded for days.* A comma in that position produces the comma splice, one of the few errors English handles unforgivingly. The semicolon is also required before a conjunctive adverb bridging two clauses: *She had the evidence; however, she lacked a hearing.* Note that *however* takes a following comma; the semicolon does the joining, the adverb only signals the turn.\n\nIts second office is humbler. In a list whose items already contain commas, semicolons act as super-commas, marking the major divisions: *The delegates came from Lima, Peru; Osaka, Japan; and Cork, Ireland.* Here the mark is pure traffic control, and no independent clauses are involved.\n\nWhat the semicolon cannot do is introduce. That is the colon's work — announcement, expansion, the list that follows a promise. Nor should it precede a coordinating conjunction joining clauses, where a comma suffices.\n\nUsed well, the semicolon signals a writer thinking in relations rather than in fragments: two thoughts held in one frame, balanced, neither subordinated to the other. It has survived five centuries on that strength alone.","ttft_ms":11595,"ttft_stream_ms":3347,"time_to_request_ms":30,"type":"result","duration_ms":22315,"uuid":"11111111-1111-4111-8111-000000000010"}
